## Caddy edge for the local-public dashboard demo
##
## T7.1 of plan 2026-05-29-dashboard-public-integration-canonicalization.
##
## Topology (droplet 165.227.121.20 OR any host with public DNS pointing at it):
##
##   browser → Cloudflare DNS (A record) → droplet :80/:443
##                                          → Caddy (this file)
##                                            → localhost:5173  (vite dev server)
##                                            → localhost:3002 (loopzero-cloud via port-forward)
##                                            → localhost:14433 (ory-kratos-public via port-forward)
##
## Auto-HTTPS via Let's Encrypt HTTP-01 challenge on :80.
## Certs persist under /var/lib/caddy by default (or $XDG_DATA_HOME).
##
## Start with: `task cloud:caddy:up` (binds :80/:443, requires sudo).
## Stop with:  `task cloud:caddy:down`.
##
## Required prereqs:
##   - Caddy >= 2.7 installed
##   - Cloudflare DNS A records pointing app/cloud/auth.loopzero.example at the host
##   - `task cloud:port-forward:up` to expose loopzero-cloud + ory-kratos locally

{
  email admin@loopzero.example

  # T1.2 of plan kratos-self-service-rate-limit-systemic v1.1 (EC-2 absorption +
  # F-sec-4 absorption — also pin CF IPv6 ranges).
  # Caddy lives behind Cloudflare Tunnel for auth-dev.loopzero.example — `{remote_host}`
  # resolves to CF's internal hop address, NOT the real end-user IP. Without
  # `trusted_proxies` Caddy refuses to honor the `CF-Connecting-IP` header that
  # CF forwards, so every rate_limit zone keyed on that header would silently
  # share ONE bucket across ALL CF-fronted users (FAANG-grade gotcha per
  # blueprint Q2). CF's published IPv4 + IPv6 ranges (https://www.cloudflare.com/ips/)
  # are pinned verbatim below. Annual refresh tracked in operator runbook.
  # F-sec-4: IPv6 list closes the bypass where an IPv6 CF hop would otherwise
  # be untrusted and every rate-limit bucket would collapse to one (or fall
  # back to `{remote_host}` semantics).
  servers {
    trusted_proxies static \
      173.245.48.0/20 \
      103.21.244.0/22 \
      103.22.200.0/22 \
      103.31.4.0/22 \
      141.101.64.0/18 \
      108.162.192.0/18 \
      190.93.240.0/20 \
      188.114.96.0/20 \
      197.234.240.0/22 \
      198.41.128.0/17 \
      162.158.0.0/15 \
      104.16.0.0/13 \
      104.24.0.0/14 \
      172.64.0.0/13 \
      131.0.72.0/22 \
      2400:cb00::/32 \
      2606:4700::/32 \
      2803:f800::/32 \
      2405:b500::/32 \
      2405:8100::/32 \
      2a06:98c0::/29 \
      2c0f:f248::/32
  }
}

# ============================================================================
# The hosts WITHOUT a suffix (`app|cloud|auth.loopzero.example`) were REMOVED on 2026-08-19 (B-025).
#
# They were described as "legacy, reserved for prod cutover", but they forwarded to the
# SAME local ports as the `-dev` blocks below: 5173 (Vite), 3002 and 14433. In other words,
# three production hostnames serving development instances — the identity one among them.
# Measured live: `cloud` returned 401 and `auth` 200, both of them LIVE.
#
# They did not serve the cutover that justified keeping them: a real cutover would point at
# production upstreams, not at dev ports on the operator's machine — they would have had to be
# rewritten anyway. Meanwhile, `app.loopzero.example` was one `npm run dev` away from serving the
# dev-server on the internet under a production name, which is what the Red Team already closed
# twice (loopzero-cloud#66 and #141).
#
# Record: knowledge-base/decisions/MEET-ASYNC-AMENDMENT-2026-08-19-remove-legacy-prod-hostnames.md
# ============================================================================

# ============================================================================
# DEV-PUBLIC env (plan 2026-06-04-dev-public-domain-canonicalization, T1.1)
# ============================================================================
# Mirrors the auth/app/cloud blocks above on *-dev.loopzero.example so dev test
# infra can coexist with the legacy *.loopzero.example (reserved for prod cutover).
# Cookie domain = loopzero.example — shared by both env so dashboard cross-host
# session works identically. OAuth callbacks for the dev OAuth apps go to
# https://auth-dev.loopzero.example/self-service/methods/oidc/callback/{provider}.

app-dev.loopzero.example {
  # Plan release-blockers-2026-06-25-set T1.1 + T2.1 (closes #44+#45+#47).
  # 4-header response set (X-Frame DENY + nosniff + Referrer + HSTS) applied at
  # every response from this host block. Closes #44 clickjacking + #45 nosniff/
  # Referrer/HSTS gap on the SPA.
  #
  # Plan caddyfile-app-dev-prod-bundle-file-server-canonical (2026-06-26):
  # closes Red Team loopzero-cloud#66 (Vite source TypeScript publicly served).
  # Prior plan caddyfile-csp-vite-dev-vs-prod-bundle v1.1 ADR D2 was wrong —
  # it argued the operator-local Caddy was non-public, removed Vite-path 404
  # guards + kept the Vite dev reverse-proxy. Reality: this Caddy IS public
  # via Cloudflare DNS, so Vite dev source TypeScript + HMR runtime +
  # node_modules cache leaked publicly. Per blueprint Option B canonical-
  # shipped — switched to file_server serving dashboard/dist (prod-bundle);
  # those Vite source files are NEVER reachable publicly because they don't
  # exist in the served root. CSP restored matching dashboard/nginx.conf
  # canonical shape. Operator workflow: cd loopzero-cloud/dashboard && npm run
  # build before task cloud:caddy:up. The Taskfile target cloud:caddy:up
  # exports DASHBOARD_DIST automatically to the workspace sibling repo
  # loopzero-cloud/dashboard/dist (per the sibling layout <REPO_PARENT>/{loopzero,loopzero-cloud,
  # loopzero-cli,loopzero-contracts} workspace layout). Override DASHBOARD_DIST
  # before task invocation if you have a non-standard layout.
  # See docs/runbooks.md.
  # Red Team loopzero-cloud#86 closure path normalization is enforced at the
  # loopzero-cloud BFF layer (cloud/internal/middleware/path_normalize.go)
  # because Caddy's `path_regexp` matcher operates on the CLEANED path
  # (consecutive slashes already collapsed by Caddy's URL parser before
  # routing). A Caddyfile-side rewrite cannot detect `//engine/*` here
  # — it would route to `handle /engine/*` correctly only when the
  # matcher saw the raw URI, which it does not. The BFF middleware
  # uses Go's `r.RequestURI` (preserves raw double-slash), detects
  # consecutive slashes BEFORE auth middleware, and emits 308 redirect
  # to the canonical single-slash form.

  header {
    X-Frame-Options "DENY"
    X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
    Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
    # CSP MUST be a superset of dashboard/index.html <meta> CSP — browsers
    # enforce the STRICTER intersection of header CSP vs meta CSP (WHATWG
    # fetch spec). The meta CSP is the dashboard team's canonical contract:
    # script-src 'unsafe-inline' (PWA service-worker register inline),
    # style-src + font-src allowlist Google Fonts (Geist family), connect-src
    # allowlist Stripe checkout. frame-ancestors is header-only (not valid
    # in meta) so it's added here. Caught LIVE 2026-06-26 by Red Team Chrome
    # devtools when prior version stripped Google Fonts via narrower header
    # CSP intersection. Plan caddyfile-app-dev-prod-bundle-file-server-
    # canonical v1.2.
    Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' data: https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; connect-src 'self' ws: wss: https://checkout.stripe.com https://*.stripe.com https://auth-dev.loopzero.example; form-action 'self' https://auth-dev.loopzero.example https://accounts.google.com https://github.com; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
    Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
  }

  # CANONICAL CADDY V2 SPA + API PROXY PATTERN (v1.3 — 2026-06-26):
  # Explicit `handle` blocks for API/auth proxies MUST come BEFORE the
  # catch-all `handle {}` block. Caddy v2 evaluates `handle` blocks in
  # source order and the FIRST match wins; an unnamed `handle {}` matches
  # everything not previously matched. Without this nesting, `try_files`
  # (which is in the "rewrite" directive group and runs BEFORE the routing
  # phase) was rewriting EVERY non-existent path to /index.html — including
  # /sessions/whoami and /v1/auth/me — so the reverse-proxies never saw
  # them and the dashboard JSON.parse'd SPA HTML as auth responses (LIVE
  # caught 2026-06-26 by Red Team Chrome devtools: `[auth] getSession:
  # network/parse error SyntaxError: Unexpected token '<', "<!DOCTYPE "
  # ... is not valid JSON`). The fix wraps file_server + try_files in the
  # catch-all `handle {}` so the rewrite ONLY applies to paths that fell
  # through every explicit handle — i.e., dashboard SPA routes, not API
  # paths. Plan caddyfile-app-dev-prod-bundle-file-server-canonical v1.3.

  # loopzero-cloud#141 (SECURITY) defense-in-depth — explicit deny of
  # dev-server-only paths that MUST NEVER be reachable on the public
  # edge, even if a stale/dev build accidentally landed in DASHBOARD_DIST.
  # Blocks source (/src/*), Vite runtime (/@vite/*, /@react-refresh),
  # node_modules, config files (vite.config.*, tsconfig.*, package.json,
  # package-lock.json), and secret env (/.env, /.env.*). Returns 404 (not
  # 403) so the response gives no signal that the path was blocked —
  # matches the "path does not exist" shape a prod-only build would return.
  # Ordered before session/BFF handlers so nothing downstream can bypass.
  @dev_server_only {
    path /src/* /@vite/* /@react-refresh /node_modules/* /vite.config.* /tsconfig.* /package.json /package-lock.json /.env /.env.*
  }
  respond @dev_server_only 404

  # Narrowed from /sessions/* to the single endpoint the dashboard calls
  # (measured 2026-08-06 against loopzero-cloud/dashboard/src: 6 call sites, all
  # /sessions/whoami). The wildcard also exposed Kratos's session LIST and
  # session DELETE on the dashboard origin, which no client uses. Logout is
  # unaffected — it lives under /self-service/logout, handled below.
  #
  # This narrows the SURFACE, not the payload: whoami still returns the whole
  # Ory session, including devices[].ip_address and recovery_addresses that no
  # client reads. Filtering the body needs either a Caddy response-rewrite
  # module or a BFF endpoint; tracked as an open finding, and declared as such
  # in infra/quality/proxy-exposure.txt.
  handle /sessions/whoami {
    reverse_proxy localhost:14433 {
      header_up Host auth-dev.loopzero.example
    }
  }
  # Everything else under /sessions/ is denied explicitly. Without this block
  # the narrowed path above would let those requests fall through to the SPA
  # catch-all and answer `<!DOCTYPE html>` to a JSON client — the Red Team #66
  # shape the caddyfile-csp gate exists to prevent. 404 rather than 403 gives
  # no signal that the path was recognised and blocked.
  handle /sessions/* {
    respond "Not Found" 404
  }
  handle /self-service/* {
    reverse_proxy localhost:14433 {
      header_up Host auth-dev.loopzero.example
    }
  }

  # Plan J-09 T1.1 (2026-06-05) — GitHub App install handshake.
  # The dashboard's "Install GitHub App" button uses a RELATIVE URL
  # (/v1/integrations/github/install?return_to=...). Without this
  # handle the request hits Vite and 404s, breaking the install flow.
  # Proxy /v1/integrations/github/* to loopzero-cloud so install + callback
  # both work from app-dev.loopzero.example. Cookies are set with Domain=
  # loopzero.example (AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN) so they travel cross-subdomain
  # back to app-dev when GitHub redirects.
  handle /v1/integrations/github/* {
    reverse_proxy localhost:3002 {
      header_up Host app-dev.loopzero.example
    }
  }

  # Data plane — loopzero-memory / loopzero-rag / loopzero-lens (#470). Each call is authorised by the
  # core cluster's loopzero-cloud (localhost:3002, its NodePort) before it reaches a service:
  # its forward-auth endpoint validates the caller's loopzero_live_* key, mints a per-tenant
  # key on the service, and answers it in `Authorization` plus the caller's identity in
  # `X-LoopZero-User-Id`; any other answer (401/403/503) goes back to the client and nothing
  # reaches the service. The path loses its product prefix (/v1/memory/x → /v1/x) and
  # goes to the service's NodePort on the core cluster (infra/k3d/core-cluster.yaml).
  #
  # Until #470 these went to a docker-compose Traefik on :8092 that authorised against a
  # second loopzero-cloud with its own user database, from directories no longer on the host.
  #
  # A client-supplied X-LoopZero-User-Id is dropped BEFORE forward_auth so only the one the
  # authoriser returns reaches the service, and `route` keeps that order: Caddy's default
  # order runs forward_auth before request_header, which would drop the identity the
  # authoriser just copied (the same trap `cell_edge` below records).
  #
  # MUST precede the /v1/* catch-all below.
  handle /v1/memory/* {
    route {
      request_header -X-LoopZero-User-Id
      forward_auth localhost:3002 {
        uri /internal/authz/forward
        copy_headers Authorization X-LoopZero-User-Id
      }
      uri path_regexp ^/v1/memory/ /v1/
      reverse_proxy localhost:18081
    }
  }
  handle /v1/rag/* {
    route {
      request_header -X-LoopZero-User-Id
      forward_auth localhost:3002 {
        uri /internal/authz/forward-rag
        copy_headers Authorization X-LoopZero-User-Id
      }
      uri path_regexp ^/v1/rag/ /v1/
      reverse_proxy localhost:18082
    }
  }
  handle /v1/lens/* {
    route {
      request_header -X-LoopZero-User-Id
      forward_auth localhost:3002 {
        uri /internal/authz/forward-lens
        copy_headers Authorization X-LoopZero-User-Id
      }
      uri path_regexp ^/v1/lens/ /v1/
      reverse_proxy localhost:18084
    }
  }

  # loopzero-cloud's /internal/* routes (forward-auth, the MCP resolve) are the authorisation
  # itself and sit outside every auth middleware; the edge above calls them on the host.
  # From the internet, /internal/authz/forward would hand any valid loopzero_live_* key a
  # per-tenant data-plane key in a response header. Nothing below proxies /internal/*
  # today; this makes it a refusal rather than an absence (#470).
  handle /internal/* {
    respond 404
  }

  # T6.2 follow-up canonical (2026-06-12 sponsor walkthrough catch): loopzero-cloud
  # API endpoints (/v1/auth/me, /v1/auth/session/token, /v1/projects, /v1/billing,
  # etc) accessed cross-origin from dashboard would require CORS, but loopzero-cloud
  # has CORS disabled per MEET-040 (same-origin assumption). Mirror the existing
  # /sessions/* + /self-service/* + /v1/integrations/github/* same-origin pattern
  # for ALL /v1/* paths so dashboard fetches against relative URLs travel cookies
  # canonical + CORS not needed. Aligns dev-public arch with MEET-040 via edge.
  handle /v1/* {
    reverse_proxy localhost:3002 {
      header_up Host app-dev.loopzero.example
    }
  }

  # Red Team loopzero-cloud#112 closure (2026-06-28) — Hydra consent / device-flow
  # routes wired in loopzero-cloud cmd/main.go:542-545 (consent.Login + LoginComplete +
  # Consent + DeviceVerify). The CLI device-flow lands the operator at
  # https://app-dev.loopzero.example/auth/login?login_challenge=<id>; without this
  # handle the request fell through to the SPA file_server catch-all and React
  # Router rendered "Page not found" — BLOCKER for `loopzero login --device-flow`,
  # which was preventing the deploy chain end-to-end (P0 mandate 2026-06-28).
  # Mirror the /v1/* shape (same Host rewrite + same upstream).
  #
  # Red Team loopzero/loopzero#162 (2026-07-01): the previous `handle /auth/*` captured
  # EVERY path under /auth/*, but the BFF only registers the 4 OAuth-flow routes
  # below. Post-signup redirects to /auth/check-email (register_proxy.go:98) +
  # email verify link at /auth/verify hit the BFF and returned 404 Caddy raw
  # ("page not found") — signup funnel broken. Fix: named matcher @auth_bff
  # restricts the proxy to the 4 real BFF routes; everything else under /auth/*
  # (check-email, verify, …) falls through to the SPA catch-all so React Router
  # can render the SPA route.
  @auth_bff path /auth/login /auth/login/complete /auth/consent /auth/device
  handle @auth_bff {
    reverse_proxy localhost:3002 {
      header_up Host app-dev.loopzero.example
    }
  }

  # Plan caddyfile-app-dev-prod-bundle-file-server-canonical v1.4 (2026-06-26
  # — closes loopzero-cloud#88): /engine/* is the canonical BFF proxy prefix the
  # dashboard (`engineFetch` in `src/lib/api.ts:197` → `cloudFetch(`/engine${path}`)`)
  # AND the LoopZero CLI (`loopzero whoami` → `GET ${LOOPZERO_URL}/engine/api/v1/auth/whoami`)
  # both use to reach the engine API via loopzero-cloud's opaque pass-through
  # (`/engine/*` route in loopzero-cloud authenticated with Ed25519 service-auth
  # per loopzero-cloud/CLAUDE.md). Without this handle, every engine call
  # (deploy, whoami, list projects, build status, …) falls through to the
  # SPA catch-all and the consumer JSON.parse's `<!DOCTYPE html>...` →
  # Red Team #88 LIVE: `loopzero whoami → "Cannot reach API"` + dashboard
  # dashboard data screens render blank. Mirror /v1/* shape (Host: cloud-dev
  # so loopzero-cloud's host-aware middleware treats this as the canonical
  # cross-origin proxy entry).
  handle /engine/* {
    reverse_proxy localhost:3002 {
      header_up Host app-dev.loopzero.example
    }
  }

  # GitHub webhook direct-to-loopzero-api (mirrors cloud-dev bloc handling for
  # /api/v1/webhooks/github). GitHub App webhook_url is registered against
  # app-dev.loopzero.example (canonical surface post-2026-06-22). Without this
  # explicit handle the POST falls through to the SPA file_server which
  # only advertises GET/HEAD → GitHub delivery attempts 405 forever.
  # HMAC-SHA256 in webhook body is validated by loopzero-api directly
  # (api/internal/github/webhook/handler.go); Caddy is the single edge
  # boundary here per the same defense-in-layers rationale as the
  # cloud-dev.loopzero.example bloc below.
  handle /api/v1/webhooks/github {
    reverse_proxy localhost:13001 {
      header_up Host loopzero-api.loopzero-system.svc.cluster.local
    }
  }

  # Red Team loopzero-cloud#100 absorption (2026-06-27) — silent MIME outage
  # on stale chunk requests. Browser tabs already-open before a deploy
  # have an HTML page that references chunk filenames (e.g.,
  # /assets/index-OLDHASH.js) that no longer exist on disk. With the
  # generic catch-all handle below, those .js requests fall through
  # `try_files {path} /index.html` and the server returns
  # `200 + text/html + nosniff` for a .js URL → strict-MIME rejection
  # → blank screen for every logged-in user after every deploy. The
  # silent version of #95.
  #
  # Fix: dedicated /assets/* handle that DOES NOT have try_files
  # fallback to /index.html. Missing chunk → genuine 404 (the canonical
  # browser cache-bust signal — Service Worker reloads index.html and
  # re-imports the new chunk hashes). Plus the canonical immutable
  # cache header for chunks (vite hashed filenames are content-addressed,
  # so cache forever is safe).
  handle /assets/* {
    root * {$LOOPZERO_DASHBOARD_DIST:../loopzero-cloud/dashboard/dist}
    encode gzip
    header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
    file_server
  }

  # Same dedicated handle for the PWA service-worker JS (Workbox emits
  # sw.js + workbox-*.js at the root, not under /assets/). Without the
  # explicit handle these would also fall through to /index.html → MIME
  # reject → PWA dies silently. Named matcher because Caddy v2's
  # `handle` directive accepts only ONE path argument (verified
  # 2026-06-27 via Caddy v2.11.3 — multi-path failed reload).
  @pwa_service_worker {
    path /sw.js /workbox-*.js
  }
  handle @pwa_service_worker {
    root * {$LOOPZERO_DASHBOARD_DIST:../loopzero-cloud/dashboard/dist}
    encode gzip
    # Service worker MUST NOT be cached — the browser checks for a new
    # SW on every visit; cache it and the user is stuck on the old
    # one forever. Workbox convention.
    header Cache-Control "no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate"
    file_server
  }

  # Catch-all: SPA prod-bundle (every path not matched above). Canonical
  # Caddy v2 SPA shape — `root` declared at the handle-block level (NOT
  # via vars placeholder, which silently failed env resolution at request
  # time on this Caddy build). try_files rewrites missing paths to
  # /index.html (SPA client-side routing fallback for real routes like
  # /projects/foo). The dedicated /assets/* handle above means JS chunks
  # are NEVER mis-served as text/html for stale requests.
  #
  # Cache-Control: no-cache on every served path → response cached by
  # the browser, but MUST revalidate (If-None-Match) on EVERY request.
  # Pairs with the immutable /assets/* shape: index.html is the
  # bootstrap that points at hashed chunks; if index.html is cached
  # forever, the user is stuck on a deploy that may have stale chunk
  # hashes. Fixed 2026-06-27 per Red Team #95 (P0 blocker — app-dev
  # returned 404 for /, /index.html, /__config.js despite the bundle
  # being present + DASHBOARD_DIST env var set) + Red Team #100
  # absorption (silent MIME outage on stale-chunk requests).
  handle {
    root * {$LOOPZERO_DASHBOARD_DIST:../loopzero-cloud/dashboard/dist}
    encode gzip
    header Cache-Control "no-cache"
    try_files {path} /index.html
    file_server
  }
}
# `cloud-dev.loopzero.example` was REMOVED on 2026-08-10.
#
# It had two halves and both were already covered here. The payload was the GitHub
# webhook, which cannot carry a JWT (GitHub signs the body with HMAC) and so went
# straight to loopzero-api; but the webhook is registered against `app-dev`
# (cmd/loopzero-ops/internal/seed/github_webhook_fixture.go:44) and the `app-dev` block above
# already has the same direct bypass. The other half was a compatibility 301 for old
# clients — which do not exist: the platform has not launched yet.
#
# What was left was a public host serving a redirect and authorized in CORS and as a
# login return URL. Auth surface granted to nobody.


auth-dev.loopzero.example {
  # Plan release-blockers-2026-06-25-set T1.3 (closes #45 — HSTS on /auth-dev).
  # Kratos/Hydra public endpoints assert HSTS at the edge.
  header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"

  # T1.2 of plan kratos-self-service-rate-limit-systemic v1.1 — Caddy edge
  # rate-limit closing Red Team loopzero-cloud#68 (login) + #89 (recovery email
  # bomb / unlimited flow creates) + defense-in-depth #43 (register).
  # Module: github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit@5625512 (bundled by
  # caddy-build-canonical.sh per T1.1 EC-1).
  #
  # All 4 zones key on `{http.request.header.cf-connecting-ip}` so per-IP
  # buckets are real-user-IP, NOT CF tunnel hop address (EC-2 absorption —
  # trusted_proxies block in global config above honors the header from CF
  # ranges only, not from spoofed hops).
  #
  # Quotas tuned conservatively to absorb legitimate retry-during-typo
  # without enabling adversarial abuse:
  #   login_throttle             10/min  (10 password-submit attempts/min/IP
  #                                       — credential-stuffing defense)
  #   register_throttle           5/min  (5 register-submit/min/IP — slows
  #                                       enumeration scanners)
  #   recovery_throttle           3/min  (3 recovery code-submit/min/IP —
  #                                       brute-force code guess defense)
  #   recovery_flow_create_throttle 5/5min (5 NEW recovery flows/5min/IP —
  #                                         caps email-bomb attack)
  #
  # Caddy sliding-window algorithm + 429 + Retry-After per blueprint Q1.
  # Red Team loopzero-cloud#68/#43 closure: the original zone path patterns
  # `/self-service/{login,registration}/methods/*` cover only Kratos's old
  # API flow submit path. The CANONICAL browser-flow submit path used by
  # the dashboard is `/self-service/{login,registration}` WITHOUT the
  # `/methods/` suffix (per `ui.action` URL emitted by the flow init).
  # Without matching both, the dashboard's actual login/register submits
  # bypass the rate-limit entirely. Each zone now matches BOTH paths.
  rate_limit {
    zone login_throttle {
      match {
        path /self-service/login/methods/* /self-service/login
      }
      key {http.request.header.cf-connecting-ip}
      window 1m
      events 10
    }
    zone register_throttle {
      match {
        path /self-service/registration/methods/* /self-service/registration
      }
      key {http.request.header.cf-connecting-ip}
      window 1m
      events 5
    }
    zone recovery_throttle {
      match {
        path /self-service/recovery/methods/* /self-service/recovery
      }
      key {http.request.header.cf-connecting-ip}
      window 1m
      events 3
    }
    zone recovery_flow_create_throttle {
      match {
        path /self-service/recovery/browser /self-service/recovery/api
      }
      key {http.request.header.cf-connecting-ip}
      window 5m
      events 5
    }
  }

  # T3.2 of plan kratos-self-service-rate-limit-systemic v1.1 (Red Team
  # post-fix-verification absorption 2026-06-27) — route the canonical
  # register submission paths through the loopzero-cloud BFF response-shape
  # unifier (T3.1). Closes Red Team loopzero-cloud#43 (account enumeration via
  # response-shape divergence — existing email + valid password → 400
  # "exists" while new email + valid password → 200 success).
  #
  # TWO submit paths exist on Kratos:
  #   - `/self-service/registration/methods/password` — Kratos API flow
  #     (old shape; covered by /v1/auth/register too on BFF directly).
  #   - `/self-service/registration` — Kratos BROWSER flow canonical (the
  #     `ui.action` URL from `GET /self-service/registration/browser`).
  #     This is the path the dashboard browser flow actually POSTs to and
  #     was the Red Team's enumeration vector.
  #
  # BOTH must route through the BFF unifier or the enumeration leak stays
  # open via the bypass path. The BFF accepts both form-encoded (browser
  # navigation, returns 303 redirect to /auth/check-email) AND JSON (API
  # client, returns canonical universal body) — both shapes are byte
  # identical between new and existing email branches.
  #
  # PLACED BEFORE the default catch-all reverse_proxy to localhost:14433
  # (Kratos public) so this prefix wins via Caddy's first-match-wins
  # semantic. Edge rate_limit register_throttle (T1.2) still applies
  # because it's evaluated at the host level before any handle.
  #
  # Split into two explicit handles (was a single `@bff_register_paths`
  # named matcher). Functional behavior is identical (both paths still
  # reverse-proxy to the loopzero-cloud BFF at localhost:3002 with the
  # canonical `Host: cloud-dev.loopzero.example` header); the explicit shape
  # is required by the `caddyfile-csp` gate's canonical detection
  # (`handle /self-service/registration/methods/password { ... }` +
  # `handle /self-service/registration { ... }` — assertions
  # `assert_register_route_proxied_to_cloud` per Red Team #43 T3.2/T3.3).
  # KISS wins over DRY here: the two-line duplication is worth
  # gate-detectability (each block is 5 lines; sum 10 vs 7 with matcher
  # + 2 blocks — the matcher form is not fundamentally shorter).
  handle /self-service/registration/methods/password {
    reverse_proxy localhost:3002 {
      header_up Host app-dev.loopzero.example
    }
  }
  handle /self-service/registration {
    reverse_proxy localhost:3002 {
      header_up Host app-dev.loopzero.example
    }
  }

  # Plan J-05 (2026-06-05) — Hydra public routes. The device-flow
  # verification_uri Hydra emits is https://auth-dev.loopzero.example/oauth2/device/verify,
  # so browser must reach Hydra (port-forward localhost:14444), not Kratos.
  # Same for the OIDC discovery document, JWKs, userinfo, and credentials
  # endpoints that OIDC clients hit on auth-dev directly. handle blocks
  # are first-match-wins, so the default Kratos proxy below catches the rest.
  handle /oauth2/* {
    reverse_proxy localhost:14444
  }
  handle /.well-known/* {
    reverse_proxy localhost:14444
  }
  handle /userinfo {
    reverse_proxy localhost:14444
  }
  handle /credentials {
    reverse_proxy localhost:14444
  }
  # The ROOT leads to login, instead of returning 404.
  #
  # Whoever types `auth-dev.loopzero.example` in a browser is a PERSON. Until 2026-08-10 they
  # got `404 page not found` in plain text, with no way forward from there: the 404 came
  # from Kratos itself, which is an identity API and serves no page at the root. The edge
  # forwarded correctly and got the 404 back.
  #
  # No product flow leads here — the uses of this host are all programmatic (API base,
  # token issuer, CORS origin) —, but a public address that leads nowhere
  # is a product defect even when nothing points at it.
  #
  # 302 and not 301: the login destination may change, and a permanent one stays in the
  # browser cache of anyone who visited once.
  handle / {
    redir https://app-dev.loopzero.example/login 302
  }

  handle /admin/* {
    # Hydra admin should NEVER be exposed publicly; deny defense-in-depth.
    respond "Not Found" 404
  }
  # Default: Kratos public (self-service flows, sessions, health, ui).
  reverse_proxy localhost:14433
  encode gzip
}

# Cell edge — send an app host to the cell that runs it (#447, docs/decisions.md
# 2026-09-29 "The edge asks the engine which cell serves a host").
#
# Placement is decided once, in tenants.cell_id. The edge honours it without knowing
# anything about tenants: before proxying it asks the engine
# (GET /internal/edge/route, a bodyless subrequest) which cell serves the host, then
# maps that cell to the ingress it knows. An unknown host gets the engine's 404, an
# index that never loaded gets 503 — both relayed as-is.
#
# The two wildcards used to forward every host to cell-0's ingress, so an app placed
# on cell-1 was live on its cell and 404 at its public URL.
#
# LOOPZERO_EDGE_ROUTE_TOKEN is the bearer the engine requires; it lives in the
# `loopzero-edge-route-token` Secret (generate-secrets.sh). It is substituted when the
# config is adapted ({$...}), so the shell running `caddy reload` must export it —
# caddy-up-dev-public.sh reads it from the Secret. NEVER inline it here — this file
# is in git.
#
# The cell -> ingress map is the edge's own network knowledge: k3d publishes cell-0's
# load balancer on 8443 and cell-1's on 8444 (infra/k3d/cell-*-cluster.yaml).
(cell_edge) {
  # route keeps the written order. Caddy's default directive order runs
  # forward_auth BEFORE request_header, which deleted the cell the lookup had just
  # copied: the map fell to its default and every app answered 502.
  route {
    request_header -X-LoopZero-Cell
    forward_auth localhost:13001 {
      uri /internal/edge/route
      header_up Authorization "Bearer {$LOOPZERO_EDGE_ROUTE_TOKEN}"
      copy_headers X-LoopZero-Cell
    }
    map {header.X-LoopZero-Cell} {cell_ingress} {
      cell-0 127.0.0.1:8443
      cell-1 127.0.0.1:8444
      default 127.0.0.1:9
    }
    reverse_proxy {cell_ingress} {
      header_up Host {http.request.host.labels.3}.loopzero.local
      transport http {
        tls
        tls_insecure_skip_verify
      }
    }
  }
}

# J-23 preview-dev wildcard — Vercel-like PR previews
#
# ONE Caddy block + ONE Cloudflare wildcard A record cover EVERY future
# PR for every tenant + project automatically. Zero per-PR config.
#
# URL convention: <internal-sni>.preview-dev.loopzero.example
#   where <internal-sni> is the IngressRoute Host match the manifest
#   builder emits via appHostname(), e.g.,
#   "tn-08fa2ecedc3c7e77-sample-api-preview-pr-1".
#
# The {http.request.host.labels.3} expression extracts the first label
# from the public hostname (Caddy indexes labels RIGHT-to-LEFT, so for
# `foo.preview-dev.loopzero.example` → labels.3 = "foo"). The Host header
# forwarded to the workload Traefik LB substitutes the .loopzero.local
# suffix, matching the IngressRoute exactly.
#
# Cloudflare DNS: wildcard A record `*.preview-dev.loopzero.example` →
# 165.227.121.20 (proxied=false; preserves HTTP-01 challenge path).
#
# TLS — DNS-01 via Cloudflare (J-23 #68 of 4-task batch, 2026-06-07):
# Per-host HTTP-01 against a wildcard hits LE's "50 certs/week per
# Registered Domain" rate limit within a single dogfood session. Wildcard
# cert via DNS-01 needs the `caddy-dns/cloudflare` plugin baked into the
# binary (stock Caddy ships without DNS providers). Build path codified
# in `infra/scripts/local/caddy-build-with-cloudflare.sh` — produces
# `/usr/local/bin/caddy` with the cloudflare TXT-record updater wired.
#
# The CF API token MUST have Zone:DNS:Edit on loopzero.example (operator
# provisions via `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=…` env var — canonical name shared
# with `taskfiles/infra.yml` dev-public:dns precondition + the 5 scripts
# under `infra/scripts/{local,production,drill}/`). `task dev-public:caddy`
# reads it from the running process env. NEVER inline the token in this
# file — the Caddyfile is checked into git.
#
# Pre-condition: a Caddy build that includes caddy-dns/cloudflare. Run
#   bash infra/scripts/local/caddy-build-with-cloudflare.sh
# once per host; idempotent. `caddy list-modules | grep dns.providers`
# must show `dns.providers.cloudflare` for this block to issue certs.
#
# Red Team loopzero-cloud#127 root cause (2026-06-30 → 2026-07-01):
# the upstream was previously `https://172.18.0.10:443` — a hardcoded
# Docker network IP. Docker network 172.18.0.0/16 assigns container IPs
# via DHCP; k3d cluster containers get a new IP on every `docker restart`
# / `k3d cluster create`. The IP `172.18.0.10` drifted from
# k3d-loopzero-cell-0-serverlb to k3d-loopzero-secrets-serverlb after a restart
# and every customer-app request silently routed to VAULT → 502
# sustentado for the ENTIRE Red Team dev-public probe surface. The
# canonical stable form is `https://127.0.0.1:8443` — k3d declares the
# workload serverlb host-port mapping (443 → 8443) which does NOT drift
# across restarts. Regression-protector:
# infra/tests/check-caddyfile-no-docker-network-ip_test.sh (hermetic).
*.preview-dev.loopzero.example {
  tls {
    dns cloudflare {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
    resolvers 1.1.1.1
  }
  import cell_edge
  encode gzip
}

# J-25 prod-dev wildcard — production deploy URLs (Vercel-canonical)
# URL convention: <project>-<env>.prod-dev.loopzero.example
# Per ADR D1 of plan 2026-06-12-dashboard-journey-j25-live-url-discoverability,
# loopzero-api status.go:252 emits this format when BASE_DOMAIN=prod-dev.loopzero.example
# (configured in infra/tilt/loopzero-api-values.yaml — Tilt-canonical for
# dev-public per memory `feedback_tilt_owned_deployment_yaml_drift`).
#
# Pattern parallel to *.preview-dev.loopzero.example (J-23 delivered 2026-06-07):
# Caddy rewrites external `<project>-<env>.prod-dev.loopzero.example` →
# internal `<project>-<env>.loopzero.local` so Traefik IngressRoute matches.
# labels.3 extracts the 3rd dot-separated label from the request host:
#   `foo-production.prod-dev.loopzero.example` → labels.3 = "foo-production"
#   `kitchensink-production.prod-dev.loopzero.example` → labels.3 = "kitchensink-production"
#
# Cloudflare DNS prerequisite: wildcard A record `*.prod-dev.loopzero.example`
# → droplet IP (parallel to existing `*.preview-dev.loopzero.example` record).
# Without this DNS step, the Caddy block is unreachable from the public
# internet — operator MUST add the CF DNS record before sponsor walkthrough.
#
# TLS via Cloudflare DNS-01 challenge (LE prod). CF_API_TOKEN env var
# canonical (same as preview block above).
#
# Red Team loopzero-cloud#127 root cause (2026-06-30 → 2026-07-01): same
# as the *.preview-dev.loopzero.example block above — the previous
# `https://172.18.0.10:443` hardcoded Docker network IP drifted from
# k3d-loopzero-cell-0-serverlb to k3d-loopzero-secrets-serverlb after a docker
# restart and every customer-app request routed to VAULT → 502
# sustentado. Canonical stable form: `https://127.0.0.1:8443`.
# Regression-protector:
# infra/tests/check-caddyfile-no-docker-network-ip_test.sh (hermetic).
*.prod-dev.loopzero.example {
  tls {
    dns cloudflare {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
    resolvers 1.1.1.1
  }
  import cell_edge
  encode gzip
}
